A||||3||FieroMike||Connecting Rods
Z||000000||FieroMike||06-29-99||01:36 PM||||I have a question for people who have rebuilt their own engines.  I had a fried bearing on the Crankshaft on the Lobe for Piston #2.  It ruined the crank and I just finished putting the new crank, mains, and was getting ready to re-install the pistons.  I noticed that it also fried the bottom of the Piston Rod.  Do I have to change out ALL connecting rods?  Or just the ruined one?  The rod bearings are oversized for the crank but fit the standard size connecting rod.  Any thoughts or suggestions? Thanks!
Z||000001||batboy||06-29-99||07:59 PM||||You probably can get by with just buying one rod if this is not a super high performance engine. Since you are spending a lot of time and money on the rebuild and want it to last, I'd recommend taking the new rod and all the old rods into the machine shop and have them recondition them by checking for cracks, straightening them if neccessary, resizing the rod bores, and replacing the rod bolts. If you can afford it and if you think this engine will see some high rpm from time to time, it might be good to balance the crank, rods and pistons too. Putting new rod bolts in is very important, old bolts and nuts often have tiny hairline stress fractures. ARP bolts are the best.
Z||000002||lowCG||06-29-99||11:24 PM||||And always use "plastiguage"to check clearances if you don't happen to  have a 2-3" micrometer,and bore guage handy;one person's sloppy machine work,done in 5 minutes time could cost you hours of work,and hundreds of dollars.Don't assume anything.<br>When tightening the rod bolts,go in three steps to the required torque.
Z||000003||FieroMike||06-30-99||06:48 AM||||Thanks guys!!  I went ahead and put brand new ones in, I figured I have the whole thing apart I might as well!  Thanks for the tips on torquing and the rod bolts!  Got the rings and pistons in late last night. Thanks again.<p>-Mike
